      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2012 AAGL. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "STUDY OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To evaluate the clinical and surgical features of thoracic endometriosis syndrome (TES) represented by catamenial pneumothorax and pelvic endometriosis and to describe their association with infertility."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "Retrospective clinical study (Canadian Task Force classification: II-2)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "Academic tertiary referral endometriosis center."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PATIENTS",
            "content": "Seven patients who had undergone surgery because of thoracic and pelvic endometriosis between 2004 and 2010."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
            "content": "Thoracic and pelvic exploration and treatment and fertility treatment."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS",
            "content": "Concurrently, thoracic and pelvic endometriosis, and subsequent fertility were determined during long-term follow-up after combined surgical and pharmacologic interventions. The most frequent thoracic finding was diaphragmatic implants. Except for 1 patient with recurrent pleurodesis at 1 month after video-assisted thoracoscopy, no patients experienced recurrence of thoracic symptoms during a median (range) follow-up of 31.4 (1-61) months. Severe pelvic endometriosis was diagnosed in 6 patients with notable urologic and gastrointestinal tract involvement. Only 1 patient with catamenial pneumothorax had no macroscopic pelvic lesions. There was a considerable delay between the onset of pelvic symptoms and diagnosis. Median postoperative follow-up was 31.5 (7-84) months. Most patients who tried to conceive had primary infertility."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Thoracic endometriosis syndrome, represented by catamenial pneumothorax, was strongly associated with severe pelvic endometriosis and a high rate of infertility. Inasmuch as many patients with thoracic endometriosis syndrome are treated by thoracic surgeons using video-assisted thoracoscopy, it is desirable to involve an experienced gynecologist who can diagnose and treat concurrent pelvic endometriosis."
          }
        ]
      },
